Fillets: a rounding whose contact runs off the face of its edge across a
sharp convex edge rolls on that edge (mitcad#121): e.g. the edge between a
bevel and a slot's wall rounded wider than the bevel, or the foot of a
wall whose lowest strip is lower than the rounding, the wall leaning back
above it.

ChFi3d_Builder::StartSolOverflow (0011, 0013): where the contact on one
side lies beyond the far edge of its face (the face of the spine's edge),
the side moved on across that edge, and where a ball touched the face
beyond (and the other side's face) clear of the face passed, the blend
ran onto the face beyond; only where no ball touched it did the side roll
on the edge. Where that edge is a sharp convex edge of the solid, the
blend now rolls on it whether or not a ball touches the face beyond: it
stays tangent to the other side's face and runs through the edge, which
is the rounding that consumes the narrow face and leaves the face beyond
as it is. The ball touching the face beyond rounded the corner as if the
narrow face were not there (a larger rounding that cuts into the face
beyond, and whose corners at the spine's ends found no face at the end:
IntersectionAtEnd, the max number of faces reached). Not where the edge
is tangent or concave (the face beyond rises into the ball's way: it
runs onto that face as before), not where the edge is rounded by the same
fillet (its rounding takes the edge away: the two blends overlap, 0013),
and not where the contact passes more faces before it reaches one it
touches (OCCT's handling of several narrow faces is kept).
